Hello Yocto community,

I wanted to share a small tool I have been working on called *VulnTrack*.

VulnTrack is a lightweight, browser-based dashboard for reviewing Yocto vul=
nerability data and SBOM/SPDX files. It helps make Yocto-generated security=
 data easier to inspect, understand, and share.

GitHub:
https://github.com/prashantdivate/VulnTrack

Live demo:
https://prashantdivate.github.io/VulnTrack/

The basic idea is simple: instead of manually opening large JSON files and =
going through long package entries, CVE strings, patch status fields, vulne=
rability summaries, and SBOM component data, VulnTrack provides a visual da=
shboard on top of that information.

It currently supports:

*=20

Yocto cve-summary.json review

*=20

Yocto-generated SBOM/SPDX file review

*=20

Package-level vulnerability analysis

*=20

CVE severity and status summaries

*=20

Affected package drill-down

*=20

SBOM component inspection

*=20

HTML, PDF, Markdown, JSON, and CSV report exports

The exported reports include useful sections such as executive summary, sev=
erity/status charts, priority CVEs, package risk summary, and remediation-f=
ocused findings.

I know the first question may be: why another tool, when Yocto/OpenEmbedded=
 already has cve-check , patchtest, VulnScout, and other security-related t=
ooling?

VulnTrack is not intended to replace those tools.

The way I see it:

*=20

cve-check generates Yocto CVE data.

*=20

Yocto-generated SPDX/SBOM output provides component and software bill of ma=
terials visibility.

*=20

patchtest and existing Yocto/OE workflows help with patch validation and de=
velopment process quality.

*=20

VulnScout and related efforts help with vulnerability monitoring and securi=
ty workflows.

*=20

VulnTrack sits after this data is generated and focuses on human review, vi=
sualization, and report export.

*So the goal is not to duplicate scanning, patch validation, or vulnerabili=
ty detection. The goal is to make already-generated Yocto CVE and SBOM data=
 easier to review, explain, share, and document.*

This can be useful when security data needs to be discussed with embedded L=
inux engineers, product security teams, release teams, managers, customers,=
 or compliance stakeholders who may not want to inspect raw JSON files dire=
ctly.

VulnTrack runs fully in the browser:

*=20

No backend

*=20

No database

*=20

No account

*=20

No vulnerability data upload

All processing is local in the browser.

Some possible use cases:

*=20

Quick review of Yocto cve-summary.json

*=20

Review of Yocto-generated SBOM/SPDX files

*=20

Release readiness vulnerability summary

*=20

Sharing CVE and SBOM status with product/security teams

*=20

Generating HTML/PDF vulnerability reports

*=20

Creating evidence for internal security review

*=20

Supporting CRA-oriented vulnerability management documentation

I would be happy to get feedback from Yocto and OpenEmbedded users:

*=20

Is this useful for your CVE or SBOM review workflow?

*=20

Are there fields from cve-summary.json or SPDX/SBOM files that should be re=
presented better?

*=20

Would comparison between two builds be useful?

*=20

Would triage notes or review decisions be useful?

*=20

Are there better ways to align this with existing Yocto/OE security workflo=
ws?

Feedback, suggestions, issues, and contributions are welcome.

Thanks,
Prashant Divate

